# 175 lines
     1	# Design constraints for a household assistant
     2	
     3	Before I let this idea become a "platform," I want to write down the constraints that make it worth building at all.
     4	
     5	If I do not do this early, the project will drift toward the usual failure modes: too much scope, too much enchantment, too much surveillance posture, too much hidden complexity, and not enough usefulness on an ordinary Wednesday.
     6	
     7	## 1. It has to reduce load, not create a new hobby
     8	
     9	The assistant is not allowed to become another dependent system that needs constant tuning, prompting, babysitting, dashboard grooming, or ritual maintenance just to justify its existence.
    10	
    11	If it saves time only after I spend three weekends integrating and curating everything, then it is a workshop project, not household infrastructure.
    12	
    13	The test is simple:
    14	
    15	- does it make this week lighter
    16	- does it help on a tired day, not just on an ambitious day
    17	- would Petra describe it as useful rather than "one more thing you are currently very interested in"
    18	
    19	That last one is brutal but fair.
    20	
    21	## 2. Local-first is the default, not the slogan
    22	
    23	Family life contains too many things that should not casually become cloud training slurry or vendor residue.
    24	
    25	School notes, kindergarten messages, household routines, health-adjacent patterns, private tensions, children's details, and all the soft context that makes a home function should stay local unless there is a very clear reason not to.
    26	
    27	This does not mean some cloud component can never exist. It means the burden of proof runs in one direction:
    28	
    29	- local by default
    30	- export intentionally
    31	- keep the sensitive raw material close to home
    32	- never pretend "temporary upload" is the same thing as no exposure
    33	
    34	The assistant should earn trust partly by where it does not send things.
    35	
    36	## 3. Household consent beats technical possibility
    37	
    38	Just because something can be sensed, transcribed, linked, summarized, or inferred does not mean it should be.
    39	
    40	I do not want to build a family panopticon decorated as convenience.
    41	
    42	That means no ambient capture just because it is available.
    43	No auto-listening room fantasy.
    44	No creepy memory model that quietly turns domestic life into permanent searchable evidence.
    45	
    46	If the household system knows something sensitive, it should be because a person deliberately placed it there for a clear reason. The assistant should help with explicit notes, chosen reminders, visible schedules, and bounded summaries more than with passive extraction.
    47	
    48	Useful beats magical.
    49	Visible beats spooky.
    50	
    51	## 4. It must preserve lane boundaries
    52	
    53	One of the biggest temptations in any "assistant" system is to merge everything because merged context looks smart.
    54	
    55	In practice, merged context is how you leak the wrong thing into the wrong lane.
    56	
    57	My life is not one context. It is overlapping contexts with different trust rules:
    58	
    59	- family and children
    60	- Petra's work and bureau-adjacent requests
    61	- my day job
    62	- consulting clients
    63	- startup experiments
    64	- private health and personal notes
    65	- hobby clutter that should not suddenly become strategic input
    66	
    67	The assistant should be able to notice relationships across those lanes without flattening them into one big context soup. It should help me navigate boundaries, not erase them.
    68	
    69	## 5. It has to be interruption-friendly
    70	
    71	This system is being designed for a life made of fragments.
    72	
    73	That means it has to work for:
    74	
    75	- ten-minute resumptions
    76	- half-finished notes
    77	- postponed tasks
    78	- "what was I doing here" moments
    79	- week plans broken by illness, school surprises, or work fires
    80	
    81	A lot of productivity tooling quietly assumes clean context and long focus blocks. That is not my life. The assistant has to help on broken terrain.
    82	
    83	If it only shines when I am already organized and well-rested, it is ornamental.
    84	
    85	## 6. Quiet competence matters more than personality
    86	
    87	I do not need a chirpy sidekick.
    88	
    89	Tone matters, but mainly in the negative sense: the assistant should not sound theatrical, manipulative, overeager, or weirdly proud of ordinary actions. It should not manufacture intimacy. It should not pressure me to engage with it like it is a pet, a coach, or a social app.
    90	
    91	The ideal tone is calm, direct, and slightly boring in the best possible way.
    92	
    93	When it helps, I should feel supported.
    94	When it is absent, I should feel nothing.
    95	
    96	That is a compliment.
    97	
    98	## 7. It must explain itself when the stakes are real
    99	
   100	Invisible automation is only pleasant while everything is low-risk.
   101	
   102	As soon as the system is crossing a boundary, making a commitment, inferring intent from messy notes, or omitting something that could matter, I need it to be legible.
   103	
   104	Not verbose. Legible.
   105	
   106	I want to know:
   107	
   108	- what it knows from source
   109	- what it inferred
   110	- where the uncertainty is
   111	- why it chose this summary, draft, or reminder
   112	- when it needs confirmation instead of pretending confidence
   113	
   114	If I cannot inspect the reasoning in important cases, then trust will decay the first time it gets something subtly wrong.
   115	
   116	## 8. Good enough beats universal
   117	
   118	I need to keep remembering that the first useful version is probably narrow and unglamorous.
   119	
   120	The goal is not "an assistant for all of life."
   121	The goal is probably something more like:
   122	
   123	- one weekly household synthesis that is actually worth reading
   124	- one reliable place for active reminders and pending follow-ups
   125	- one way to resume stalled work without rereading half my own history
   126	- one careful split between family-safe and lab-only context
   127	
   128	That is already a lot.
   129	
   130	If I try to solve voice, omnichannel capture, generalized planning, family displays, agentic action, and productization all at once, I will build a shrine to scope instead of a useful system.
   131	
   132	## 9. It should improve the real week, not the imagined future week
   133	
   134	I am unusually vulnerable to elegant future systems.
   135	
   136	I can always picture the integrated version: better routing, smarter memory, cleaner dashboards, structured entities, ambient surfaces, graceful summaries, all of it.
   137	
   138	That future picture is not useless, but it is dangerous.
   139	
   140	The real question is more grounded:
   141	
   142	- what helps this week
   143	- what helps when someone is sick
   144	- what helps when Petra needs something fast
   145	- what helps when work is noisy and I have twenty minutes
   146	- what still works when I am too tired to appreciate the architecture
   147	
   148	If a feature only pays off in the imaginary life where I have spare cognitive bandwidth and uninterrupted weekends, it belongs on the parking lot, not in the assistant core.
   149	
   150	## 10. The assistant should make me more present, not more optimized
   151	
   152	This is the hardest one to evaluate and probably the most important.
   153	
   154	The system is succeeding if it helps me keep promises, protect attention, and stop leaking important obligations through fatigue.
   155	
   156	It is failing if it turns my family into a logistics problem, my hobbies into productivity units, or my own body into a dashboard with guilt attached.
   157	
   158	I want leverage, not colonization.
   159	
   160	That is the line.
   161	
   162	## Working definition
   163	
   164	For now, the shortest honest definition I have is this:
   165	
   166	The assistant is a local-first continuity system for a busy household and overloaded working life.
   167	
   168	It should help me remember, resume, separate, and follow through.
   169	
   170	It should not perform omniscience.
   171	It should not flatten trust boundaries.
   172	It should not demand to become the center of the house.
   173	
   174	If it becomes calm infrastructure, it has a future.
   175	If it becomes another clever appetite, it does not.

