# 78 lines
     1	# Why I keep coming back to a personal assistant
     2	
     3	I do not actually want a robot lifestyle.
     4	
     5	What I want is to stop paying the same stupid attention tax over and over again.
     6	
     7	Too much of my week is consumed by reloading context I already earned once. What did I promise that client, which school note mattered, why did I postpone that print, what exactly did Petra ask for, which half-finished product thought still feels true after three bad nights of sleep, what bit of operational pain at work is worth extracting into a real pattern instead of solving one more time with adrenaline and memory.
     8	
     9	None of those are individually dramatic. Together they create the feeling that my life is made of open loops that keep breeding in the dark.
    10	
    11	That is the real backdrop for this assistant idea.
    12	
    13	It is not some abstract conviction that AI will change everything. Maybe it will, maybe it will not, and most of the discourse around that question is unbearable anyway. My version is smaller and more practical. I have a house full of actual obligations, a day job that rewards follow-through more than theory, side work that only matters if it survives billing and support, and a startup ambition that will die of fragmentation if I do not build better leverage around myself.
    14	
    15	I keep noticing the same pattern in every lane:
    16	
    17	- the hard part is often not intelligence in the grand sense
    18	- the hard part is remembering the right thing at the right moment
    19	- the hard part is preserving boundaries between lanes without losing useful context
    20	- the hard part is reducing friction without turning life into an overdesigned cockpit
    21	
    22	I do not need an omniscient machine. I need a calm one.
    23	
    24	I need something that can help me hold continuity across interrupted days. Something that notices the difference between "important later" and "urgent now." Something that helps me resume instead of restart. Something that can draft the boring message, surface the previous decision, remind me that the printer problem is not the same thing as a printer-upgrade opportunity, and tell me when the household is about to be surprised by a school or kindergarten obligation that was visible all along but living in the wrong place.
    25	
    26	The household piece matters more than I expected.
    27	
    28	Before kids, a lot of overload could be solved by brute force and one late evening. That stops working once other people's rhythms are involved. School has windows. Kindergarten has windows. Sick days reorder the whole week. A bureau deadline spills into dinner. A dog walk is not "miscellaneous," it is part of whether my head works. A note about a costume day can matter more to the actual quality of the week than one more elegant architecture sketch.
    29	
    30	So the assistant idea keeps pulling away from the usual "smart chatbot" framing and toward something closer to domestic infrastructure.
    31	
    32	Not visible all the time. Not talking unless useful. Not pretending to be a person. Not nudging for engagement. Just quietly good at continuity, recall, triage, and context.
    33	
    34	That sounds modest, but I think it is harder than the flashy version.
    35	
    36	The flashy version can fake usefulness with tone and breadth. The infrastructure version has to earn trust. It has to be right often enough, legible when it is not, and quiet enough that the household does not experience it as one more demand machine.
    37	
    38	Part of the reason I care so much is that I am also designing for the version of myself I actually have, not the heroic founder version from startup mythology.
    39	
    40	I do not have uninterrupted strategic days.
    41	I have fragments.
    42	
    43	I have half an hour before the house wakes up.
    44	I have ten minutes between meetings.
    45	I have an evening window that may disappear because one child spikes a fever or Petra needs help finishing a model part.
    46	I have weekends that can either produce real progress or vanish into logistics.
    47	
    48	That means any assistant worth keeping has to work with interrupted cognition. It has to respect partial progress. It has to make resumed work cheaper. It has to preserve enough local history that I do not spend my best energy recreating yesterday's mental state.
    49	
    50	I also suspect that if I can make something genuinely useful for my own life, it will teach me more than another polished product theory deck ever could.
    51	
    52	Small teams, overloaded operators, parents, founders, consultants, and everyone doing too many cross-functional jobs all seem to suffer from the same broad disease: not a shortage of raw information, but a shortage of durable working continuity.
    53	
    54	People do not just need answers.
    55	They need fewer restarts.
    56	
    57	That feels like the wedge worth caring about.
    58	
    59	There is also a quieter emotional truth in here that I should probably admit plainly.
    60	
    61	I am not trying to automate the people in my life.
    62	I am trying to protect my ability to show up for them without running on guilt and improvisation all the time.
    63	
    64	If a machine can help me remember what matters, recover my place after interruption, keep promises straighter, and reduce the amount of household and work coordination that currently lives in my skull, then that is not a gadget project. That is quality-of-life infrastructure.
    65	
    66	The bar, though, is high.
    67	
    68	If this thing becomes needy, creepy, overconfident, cloud-greedy, or generally enchanted with its own cleverness, then it fails the brief. If it turns family life into telemetry, it fails. If it gives me one more dashboard that I maintain for the privilege of being told I am behind on my own life, it fails. If it cannot survive contact with bad weeks, interruptions, ambiguity, and the fact that some notes should remain private and local, it fails.
    69	
    70	So I keep coming back to the same ambition:
    71	
    72	Build an assistant that behaves more like a reliable house system than a product demo.
    73	
    74	Something with enough memory to be useful, enough boundaries to be safe, enough taste to stay quiet, and enough humility to help without pretending it understands more than it does.
    75	
    76	That is still fuzzy. It may stay fuzzy for a while.
    77	
    78	But it is the first version of the idea that feels honest.

